  "title": "Pentagon Limits Some Canada Military Contacts While Keeping NORAD Intact",
  "deck": "U.S. officials say new guidance affects some informal engagements and leaves joint defense and intelligence cooperation in place.",
  "category": "world",
  "categoryLabel": "World",
  "type": "reporting",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-22T12:00:00Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-22T12:00:00Z",
  "body": [
    "The Pentagon is restricting some informal military engagements with Canada while keeping joint defense and intelligence cooperation in place, U.S. officials said Saturday. The clarification followed a Friday report by The Washington Post that Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth had circulated classified guidance imposing new limits on the defense relationship.",
    "Speaking at the Halifax International Security Forum, Republican Sen. Mike Rounds said the guidance largely concerns social engagements and permission to attend think-tank events. Pentagon spokesman Sean Parnell said the guidance leaves the countries’ joint NORAD command, NATO cooperation, and defense intelligence sharing intact. The full scope of the classified memo remains unclear.",
    "Canadian Defence Minister David McGuinty said he had yet to see the memo and had observed no change in military cooperation. He pointed to Canadian and U.S. vessels still working together on counter-drug operations in the Caribbean. The Pentagon had already barred its officials from attending the Halifax forum under earlier restrictions on think-tank participation. Past administrations from both parties sent senior officials, including defense secretaries.",
    "The Pentagon says it is prioritizing cooperation that advances U.S. defense goals. The practical question is whether approval requirements for informal meetings will change how often officials from the two countries meet outside their established military channels."
  ],
